\description{
Turns the personnel and contract modules into the table that models of
careers need: for each employee and each snapshot they are active in,
\strong{what happened by the next snapshot} (did they stay, retire or leave
otherwise, and for how long were they at risk) and \strong{how their real pay
changed over the following year}. Neither module records these: they
come from comparing each person's record with their own later records.

It works at any snapshot frequency (monthly, quarterly, annual or
irregular), measuring time from the dates themselves, and reports every
rate per year:
\enumerate{
\item \strong{One row per person and snapshot.} Each personnel record gets that
snapshot's total pay across all of the person's contracts, and its
covariates.
\item \strong{At risk.} Every row that is \code{"active"} and has a later snapshot in
the panel is an interval at risk, from \code{ref_date} to the next
snapshot (\code{next_ref_date}).
\item \strong{How it ended.} \code{exit_type} says whether the person was still
active at the next snapshot, had retired, or had left otherwise, and
\code{exposure} is the time at risk, in years.
\item \strong{Pay growth.} \code{pay_growth} compares pay with the same person's pay
a year later.
}

Each row is one observation for a model of exits (with \code{exit_type} as
the outcome and \code{exposure} as the time at risk) or of pay growth.
}
\details{
\strong{Time is counted in calendar months.} A month is 1/12 of a year,
whatever its number of days, so a year of monthly snapshots adds up to
exactly the same exposure as one annual interval.

\strong{Leavers count half the interval.} Someone who leaves is assumed to
leave halfway through, as in a life table. Someone missing for a while
and back later left (\code{"other"}), and starts a new \code{spell} when they come
back: some people, such as seasonal workers, leave and return often.

\strong{What "a year later" means.} The snapshot closest to one year after
\code{ref_date}, if it is within half the panel's usual spacing of snapshots
(the median gap): the same month a year later in a monthly panel, and
the next snapshot in an annual one. Pay over a shorter or longer span
would mix in the timing of payments (a 13th salary, back pay), so with
no snapshot that close (the panel's last year, or a snapshot missing),
\code{pay_growth} is \code{NA}. The person must be active a year later, but need
not have stayed throughout: a seasonal worker's pay growth counts.

\strong{Not at risk.} Rows with another status (e.g. pensioners) and rows at
the panel's last snapshot, since nothing is known about what came next.

\strong{Missing or zero pay.} A contract with missing pay is left out of the
person's total, which is \code{NA} only if all of them are missing. A person
with no pay, or zero pay, is still at risk; only their \code{pay_growth} is
\code{NA}.

\strong{Covariates that change over time} are taken at the start of each
interval: someone who changes occupation has rows in one occupation,
then the other.

\strong{Missing duration or date.} An active row with no \code{duration_col} is left out,
with a warning, but still counts as the person's next record for their
row before (and its exit still starts a new spell). A personnel row with
no \code{ref_date_col} is dropped. A contract with no matching personnel row is
ignored.

\strong{Duplicates.} A person with more than one personnel row in a snapshot
keeps one of them, with a warning: the last one in a data frame and, in
a database (where rows have no order), the first by status. The two are
the same when duplicates are exact copies.

\strong{Matches Level 1 on annual data.} Summing the rows by whole years of
tenure gives the exposed, stayers and pay growth of
\code{\link[=estimate_progression_rates]{estimate_progression_rates()}}.

\strong{On a database.} For database tables, the same steps run inside the
database as SQL, giving the same panel, and the result is returned
lazily. A few small queries run straight away: the snapshot dates (whose
calendar is worked out in R and sent back inline, so no write access is
needed), and counts for the warnings about duplicates and missing duration
values.

Errors if \code{duration_col} is not in \code{personnel}; if a required column is
missing from either table; if a covariate is in both tables or in
neither; if \code{personnel} has fewer than 2 snapshots; if \code{personnel} is a
database table and \code{contracts} is not a table on the same connection; or
if \code{personnel} is neither a data frame nor a database table.
}
